WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.350
- So, when did you decide
to give up teaching though

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:03.299
and just do comedy?

00:00:03.299 --> 00:00:05.160
Was it, obviously, it
was always your dream?

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.370
- It was always my dream, and it was what

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:07.540
I was working hard at.
- Yeah.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:09.890
- And I was already a young father,

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.652
and I had, my daughter was
already six months old,

00:00:12.652 --> 00:00:15.920
and, you know, I lived an
hour outside of Manhattan.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.090
There's Jenny right there.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:20.170
And I was living an hour
outside of Manhattan

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.750
in my hometown of Long Beach, New York.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:25.740
And it was a real
struggle to be able to get

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.510
to the clubs and then come back and so on.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.640
And then my wife, Janice, we'll be married

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.957
51 years in June, said,
- Wow.

00:00:32.957 --> 00:00:35.690
"I'm gonna go back to work.
(audience claps)

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:36.860
I'm gonna go back to work."

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:37.880
She had a really good job,

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:39.800
an assistant to the dean of theater

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.550
at a junior college on the island.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.047
And we had health benefits, and she said,

00:00:44.047 --> 00:00:44.880
"You know what?

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.060
You take care of Jenny all day.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:50.210
I'll come home from
the school around 5:00,

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:52.200
hand her off, do whatever you have to do,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.360
and go into the city
and become the comedian

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.090
I know that you can be."

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:59.380
And it was the greatest, greatest gift

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.550
of her selflessness as a young mother

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:04.730
with a six month old to trust me.

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:08.100
And I was Mr. Mom way
before it was popular.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.880
You know, I was, so this was 1973

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.700
and a little bit, and it was really,

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:16.930
I look at it as a real
turning point in my life,

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:17.850
not only as a father,

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.430
but as a human being
- Yeah.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:20.950
- because it gave me perspective

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:23.400
on what we're here for

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.420
and what you're responsible for,

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:29.040
and to love someone so
selflessly and take care of her

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.430
and make sure that she's
healthy and happy and educated.

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:34.840
And even in those early days,
and it forever bonded us.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.800
I mean, it was a really great,
important time in my life.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:38.633
- Wow.

00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:40.370
And what an amazing, I mean, now I get,

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.410
congratulations by the way, 51 years,

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:43.566
that's insane
(audience claps and cheers)

00:01:43.566 --> 00:01:46.540
in any environment, but especially

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:47.910
in the entertainment industry,

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.350
that's kind of unheard of.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.450
So that's amazing, but I love,

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.960
I guess the lesson there
too is just the support was,

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:54.900
you know, always there,

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.370
and the partnership was always there.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:57.672
Do you think that that's basically

00:01:57.672 --> 00:01:59.530
how that, it's lasted so long?

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:03.110
What's your advice for
staying married 51 years?

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.770
- Have a girlfriend.

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:09.173
- Oh, you know what?
(Kelly and audience laughs)

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:13.100
- The thing is that Kelly, then we laugh.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:17.000
We joke, we still learn from each other.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.160
We still love being together.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.790
This, you know, this year
was such a test of that.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:23.560
I mean, I've been in this chair

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.158
since last March 12th basically.

00:02:26.158 --> 00:02:28.140
(Kelly and Billy laugh)

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.610
And just, we just have so much fun

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.840
and keep discovering new
things about each other.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.250
And I think that's the
most important thing is

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:35.974
to still always be curious

00:02:35.974 --> 00:02:38.470
and find out more about each other.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:40.410
- Yeah, oh, well, you are lucky.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.810
That's a beautiful
relationship that you've had.

